[Music]  planets  they are home to super storms they're  home to oceans of magma  and in earth's case they're home to life  some of humanity's greatest thinkers  have dedicated their lives to studying  these awesome places  hi i'm rich burnett for one dream and in  this episode of perspectives we're going  to hear from a handful of scientists  they're going to share their favorite  facts about planets in our solar system  and beyond we're going to begin our  journey on mercury  as we learn about just how difficult it  is to match its trajectory  as it speeds around the sun enjoy  we studied earth from space and compared  our planet with all the other worlds in  our solar system  all of these missions together have made  it possible  to assemble what can be called a field  guide to the planets  and other bodies in our solar system we  now know  vicariously from our spacecraft a great  deal about what it is like  to visit these worlds  mercury is a planet of extremes it's the  smallest planet in the solar system and  the closest planet to the sun  it travels around the sun much faster  than any other planet  60 percent faster than earth at average  speeds of over 100  000 miles per hour even getting into  orbit around mercury is unusually  challenging that's because spacecraft as  well as comets or  anything else speed up as they get  closer to the sun  the force of gravity causes everything  to speed up as it approaches the sun  and moves deeper into what we call the  sun's gravity well  similarly objects falling to the surface  of the earth speed up as they get deeper  in earth's gravity well  so if you want to send a spacecraft to  mercury the big issue  is slowing down the next planet in our  solar system  is venus venus is also a rocky planet  but unlike mercury venus has a thick  atmosphere  of carbon dioxide which blankets the  surface and causes it to heat up  to over 850 degrees fahrenheit  it's an exaggerated version of the  greenhouse effect that we're concerned  about here on the earth  venus also has clouds made of sulfuric  acid  so even though the ancient greeks and  romans saw venus as the goddess of  love with all that fire and brimstone  it's  really more like the traditional concept  of hell  there's no clear-cut answer to where  earth ends  and space begins but there are ways we  can define  boundaries between earth and the rest of  the solar system  it could be based on the altitude at  which the atmosphere is so sparse that  it no longer behaves like a gas  that's called the thermopause or  where the earth's magnetic field no  longer shields us from the solar wind  that's the magnetopause in terms of  gravity  the boundary might be the distance from  earth where the sun's gravity  is stronger than the earth's gravity  that's about  1.5 million kilometers from earth at the  boundary of its gravitational hill  sphere  but this definition would make the moon  part of earth  and not in space which is not how we  think of it  governments and other organizations have  tried to define a particular altitude  above earth's surface  known as the carmen line that signals  the beginning of outer space  but of course they don't all agree on  the specific altitude  of the carmen line the united states for  example  uses 80 kilometers it's set a little  higher  at 100 kilometers by the world body  governing air sports  the federation international  earth is our only frame of reference for  a habitable planet  but even here we find life forms  surviving and  thriving in shockingly harsh conditions  we've discovered organisms that breathe  sulfur and bacteria that can survive  extreme gravity  or live a half mile deep in an antarctic  lake  eyeless creatures live out their entire  lives in pitch dark  caves and microscopic creatures known as  tardigrades  have even survived exposures to the  vacuum of space  but despite all these differences the  one  universal need of all living things on  earth  is liquid water water is therefore seen  as a non-negotiable requirement for  supporting life  sci-fi going all the way back to h.g  wells has focused on scenarios where  alien creatures from mars destroy life  on earth  but mars might have very fragile and  primitive life  incapable of withstanding microbes from  earth  we might be far more deadly to any  actual martians than they are to us  fortunately planetary protection officer  is a job at nasa  in fact there's an entire office of  planetary protection  the office works to ensure spacecraft  are as sterile as possible  when we send them to mars and other  planets because we don't want to  contaminate them  the planetary protection office also  makes sure flight plans prevent  spacecraft from colliding with  unintended planetary bodies  and of course they also ensure we don't  contaminate the earth with  extraterrestrial life when we bring back  samples from other planets  jupiter at five times the earth's sun  distance orbits the sun every  12 earth years even so the jupiter day  is short  only about 10 hours because jupiter  rotates on its axis  really fast that rapid rotation causes  the planet to bulge  about its middle and you can see that  phenomenon in photographs of the planet  and it also causes really extreme  weather  that's because the circumference of  jupiter at the equator is more than  430 000 kilometers  so the atmosphere at the equator has to  travel about 43 000 kilometers per hour  relative to the atmosphere at the poles  and as a consequence  the fast rotation breaks jupiter's thick  atmosphere into fast-moving bands and  swirls which are easily seen as a  sequence of lighter  and darker stripes parallel to jupiter's  equator  jupiter also experiences extreme wind  shearing that creates  immense vortices cyclones many times the  diameter of earth that can last for many  years  with sustained winds of hundreds of  kilometers per hour  for unusual features at the poles  nothing compares to saturn  to begin with at the south pole is a  vortex  that's a giant eye of a hurricane you  can peer  right down into the hurricane this thing  is 8  000 kilometers across it's two-thirds  the size of earth  and it's ringed by tower and clouds the  winds travel clockwise at speeds of more  than 500 kilometers an hour  another strange strange feature is up at  the north  pole of saturn here the atmospheric flow  takes the shape of a hexagon  and this was a huge puzzle for  scientists until some people modeled it  as a particular jet stream of gas  moving at different speeds than the  surrounding atmosphere  and this causes the flow to break up  into  six separate little eddies little swirls  if the differences in speed were  different this could have been an  octagon or a decagon  or some other shape the outermost  planets uranus and neptune  are often called ice giants because they  are primarily composed of ices  they each have vastly more water locked  away than is found in earth  but it's important to realize that ice  in planetary science  is much broader than frozen water ice  the category called ices refers not only  to water  but also to ammonia methane and other  materials that are volatile  because they have low temperatures for  melting and evaporation  moreover while these ice materials might  be solid  they can also be liquid or gaseous the  word ice or icy  just refers to the composition of the  material  beginning in the 1780s william and  caroline herschel  began to build a systematic catalogue of  the northern night sky  for wide publication the brother sister  team  constructed telescopes together and used  them from their home in england  to painstakingly improve on past stellar  catalogs  sweeping their telescopes methodically  across the sky  and making note of both known and newly  discovered objects  the catalog became an invaluable part of  the scientific literature  listing object's positions sizes and  brightnesses  and considered how they might be  classified  their observations also turned up  numerous new objects  william discovered the planet uranus  while caroline discovered numerous  comments  her work was recognized with the gold  medal from the royal astronomical  society  an honor that wouldn't be given to a  woman again until vera rubin received it  for her work on dark matter in 1996.  the herschel's final result the new  general catalog  is still used today and many famous  astronomical objects  are identified by their ngc number  neptune's axis tilt is similar to  earth's and so the planet experiences  seasons  unfortunately for neptune watchers the  year on neptune  is 165 earth years long  that means that all the data we have  about neptune's atmosphere  whether from voyager 2 or earth-based  telescopes  is less than a quarter of a year  so the only seasonal change we've seen  on neptune so far  is the southern hemisphere change from  spring to summer  the astronomical heroes or maybe  anti-heroes  behind pluto's journey from discovery to  demotion  span more than 70 years in 1930  clyde tombaugh discovered pluto and  classified it as the ninth planet in our  solar system  in 1960 julio fernandez predicted that  an enormous and crowded belt  of small rocky objects should surround  the outside of our solar system  just at and beyond pluto's orbit  in 1992 david jewett and jane liu  discovered the first observational  evidence of this belt  the research that followed ultimately  led to pluto's demotion from planet  status in 2006  along with a significantly expanded  picture of  our solar system and its outer  boundaries  discoveries of exoplanets have  demonstrated that planets around other  stars are abundant and varied  gas giant planets orbiting extremely  close to their parent stars  called hot jupiters were the first to be  discovered  but they actually make up the minority  of exoplanets that are out there  smaller worlds are more abundant and  there's a variety  not seen in our solar system there are  rocky planets many times bigger than  earth  aptly named super earths some of these  super earths may also contain a large  fraction of water  up to about 50 percent making them more  like water worlds than  rocky worlds and there are lava worlds  rocky planets so close to their stars  that they are covered in global magma  oceans  in the coming years we are facing a  burst of incredible innovation in  astronomy  tomorrow's heroes of astronomy are hard  at work today  working on new telescopes that have been  decades in the making  and laying the groundwork for  astonishing new discoveries  that will continue to change how we see  the universe  thanks for watching if you'd like to  learn more about any of the topics in  this episode  the full list of series that these clips  came from is in the description below  you can watch all the full series now on  onedream.com and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel  turn on notifications and you'll get an  alert every time we post a new episode  of perspectives

